## Summary

Command injection is one of the most severe vulnerabilities in web applications. Unlike SQL injection which affects only the database, command injection gives attackers direct access to the operating system.

Key takeaways:
1. Never use `shell: true` with user input
2. Use `spawn()` or `execFile()` with argument arrays
3. Validate input with allowlist patterns
4. Avoid shell commands entirely when Node.js APIs exist
5. Implement timeouts and resource limits
6. Log command executions for audit trail
7. Monitor for injection patterns in real-time
8. Prepare incident response procedures

The vulnerable code demonstrated here would give attackers complete control over your server in minutes. The secure implementation makes command injection effectively impossible by never invoking a shell interpreter with untrusted input. There is no excuse for command injection vulnerabilities in modern Node.js applications.
